    "question": "\"Describe the technological and institutional reforms of agricultural development in India.\" **[3 Marks]** \"Describe the cropping pattern in India.\" **[3 Marks]**",
    "answer": "**(First Option: Technological and Institutional Reforms)**\nTo transform traditional Indian agriculture, the government introduced several key reforms:\n1. **Institutional Reforms**: Collectivisation and consolidation of holdings (*Chakbandi*) eliminated land fragmentation. The Zamindari system was abolished to give ownership rights to actual tillers.\n2. **Credit & Price Support**: Kisan Credit Card (KCC) and Personal Accident Insurance Scheme (PAIS) were launched. Minimum Support Price (MSP) was introduced to safeguard farmers against market fluctuations.\n3. **Technological Reforms**: Introduction of Green Revolution (HYV seeds, chemical fertilizers, canal/tube well irrigation) and White Revolution (Operation Flood). Weather forecasts and specialized farming bulletins were broadcasted via radio and TV.\n**(Second Option: Cropping Pattern in India)**\nIndia has three distinct cropping seasons:\n1. **Rabi Season**: Sown in winter from October to December and harvested in summer from April to June. Major crops include Wheat, Barley, Peas, Gram, and Mustard.\n2. **Kharif Season**: Sown with the onset of monsoon in June–July and harvested in September–October. Major crops include Paddy (Rice), Maize, Jowar, Bajra, Cotton, and Jute.\n3. **Zaid Season**: A short summer season between March and June. Major crops include Watermelon, Muskmelon, Cucumber, vegetables, and fodder crops.",
